"Disagreement-Based Cross-Model Routing for Implicit Video Question Answering | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2606.14723)\nciteturn43742search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: last month; @misc{saluru2026disagreementbasedcrossmodelroutingimplicit, title={Disagreement-Based Cross-Model Routing for Implicit Video Question Answering}, author={Durga Sandeep Saluru}, year={2026}, eprint={2606.14723}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.CV}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14723}, }\nSubmitted 31 May 2026\n\n# Disagreement-Based Cross-Model Routing for Implicit Video Question Answering\n\nDS\n\nDurga Sandeep Saluru\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe study multiple-choice video question answering on the ImplicitQA benchmark, where the correct answer is never explicitly shown but must be inferred from off-screen events, line-of-sight cues, causal structure, and cross-shot spatial layout. On this benchmark a single frontier video LLM already operates near its accuracy ceiling, and we observe that conventional self-consistency strategies -- majority voting across repeated samples of the same model -- can hurt rather than help, because the model's errors on hard questions are correlated. We propose disagreement-based cross-model routing, a pure inference-time procedure that requires no labels and no training. We triple-sample a native-video model (Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview) at temperature zero, exploit the genuine sample-to-sample variance of its video-processing pipeline to identify the roughly 20% subset of questions where the three samples disagree, and route only that subset to a second model from a different family (Claude Opus 4.8) that consumes uniformly sampled frames with adaptive thinking. On the 1001-question validation set with public ground truth -- our main evaluation -- the method improves AvgAcc by +1.43 over the best single sample of the primary model, with per-category gains concentrated on Motion & Trajectory (+5.49), Inferred Counting (+3.45), and Vertical Spatial Reasoning (+1.82) -- the categories most dependent on cross-shot reference resolution. The same pipeline applied to the held-out 172-question CVPR 2026 ImplicitQA challenge test set achieves 82.03 AvgAcc / 79.71 MacroAvgAcc (+1.81 over the best single sample of the primary model), confirming the validation result on an independent split.\n\nView Paper\n\n## AI Overview\n\nNo overview yet. Generating one walks through the paper's problem, method, and results.\n\nGenerate Overview\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{saluru2026disagreementbasedcrossmodelroutingimplicit, title={Disagreement-Based Cross-Model Routing for Implicit Video Question Answering}, author={Durga Sandeep Saluru}, year={2026}, eprint={2606.14723}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.CV}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14723}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPaper page - DC-Motion: Decoupling Structure and Details via Discrete-Continuous Tokens for Human Motion Generation (https://hf-p-cfw.fyan.top/papers/2606.14721)\nciteturn43742search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; arxiv:2606.14721 ... # DC-Motion: Decoupling Structure and Details via Discrete-Continuous Tokens for Human Motion Generation\n\nPapers\n\narxiv:2606.14721\n\nCopy markdown\n\n# DC-Motion: Decoupling Structure and Details via Discrete-Continuous Tokens for Human Motion Generation\n\nPublished on Jul 6\n\nUpvote -\n\nAuthors:\n\n[Button: Hequan Wang] ,\n\n[Button: Xuean Chen] ,\n\n[Button: Jiaxu Zhang] ,\n\n[Button: Zhengbo Zhang] ,\n\n[Button: Zhigang Tu]\n\n## Abstract\n\nText-to-motion generation requires modeling both global action structure and fine-grained motion dynamics from natural language. Existing approaches typically rely on either continuous diffusion models or vector-quantized discrete representations. Diffusion models generate smooth motions but lack explicit compositional structure for temporal planning, while discrete token-based methods improve controllability but compress motion into finite codebooks, losing fine-grained dynamics. We argue that this limitation stems from a representation mismatch: action semantics such as intent, phase transitions, and temporal layout are inherently discrete and compositional, whereas joint trajectories and motion dynamics are continuous and locally correlated. To address this, we propose DC-Motion, a discrete-continuous factorized framework for human motion generation. DC-Motion decomposes motion into discrete structural tokens capturing global action layout and continuous residual latents modeling fine-grained dynamics. A text-conditioned structure generator predicts discrete tokens via iterative masked modeling, and a diffusion-based residual generator produces continuous motion conditioned on the structure. Experiments on HumanML3D and KIT-ML demonstrate that DC-Motion achieves strong performance in both FID and R-Precision, outperforming representative diffusion-based and discrete-token baselines.\n\nView arXiv page View PDF Add to collection--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDC-Motion: Decoupling Semantics and Details via Discrete-Continuous Tokens for Human Motion Generation — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/dc-motion-decoupling-semantics-and-details-via-discrete)\nciteturn43742search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; # DC-Motion: Decoupling Semantics and Details via Discrete-Continuous Tokens for Human Motion Generation ...       eprint = {2606.14721},\n\n# DC-Motion: Decoupling Semantics and Details via Discrete-Continuous Tokens for Human Motion Generation\n\nHequan Wang, Jiaxu Zhang, Zhengbo Zhang, Zhigang TuarXiv 2026 · DOI\n\nBibTeX\n    \n    @misc{wang2026dc,\n      author = {Hequan Wang and Jiaxu Zhang and Zhengbo Zhang and Zhigang Tu},\n      title = {{DC-Motion: Decoupling Semantics and Details via Discrete-Continuous Tokens for Human Motion Generation}},\n      year = {2026},\n      eprint = {2606.14721},\n      archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n      url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14721},\n    }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDisagreement-Based Cross-Model Routing for Implicit Video Question Answering (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/407116743_Disagreement-Based_Cross-Model_Routing_for_Implicit_Video_Question_Answering)\nciteturn43742search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; # Disagreement-Based Cross-Model Routing for Implicit Video Question Answering ... DOI:10.48550/arXiv.2606.14723\nPreprint\n\n# Disagreement-Based Cross-Model Routing for Implicit Video Question Answering\n\n  * May 2026\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2606.14723\n\nAuthors:\n\nDurga Sandeep Saluru\n\nDurga Sandeep Saluru\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe study multiple-choice video question answering on the ImplicitQA benchmark, where the correct answer is never explicitly shown but must be inferred from off-screen events, line-of-sight cues, causal structure, and cross-shot spatial layout. On this benchmark a single frontier video LLM already operates near its accuracy ceiling, and we observe that conventional self-consistency strategies -- majority voting across repeated samples of the same model -- can hurt rather than help, because the model's errors on hard questions are correlated. We propose disagreement-based cross-model routing, a pure inference-time procedure that requires no labels and no training. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2606.14721] DC-Motion: Decoupling Structure and Details via Discrete-Continuous Tokens for Human Motion Generation (https://rt.http3.lol/index.php?q=aHR0cHM6Ly9hcnhpdi5vcmcvYWJzLzI2MDYuMTQ3MjE)\nciteturn43742search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; View a PDF of the paper titled DC-Motion: Decoupling Structure and Details via Discrete-Continuous Tokens for Human Motion Generation, by Hequan Wang and 4 other authors ... Cite as:  | arXiv:2606.14721 [cs.GR]\n\n# Computer Science > Graphics\n\n[Submitted on 28 May 2026 (v1), last revised 6 Jul 2026 (this version, v2)]\n\n# Title:DC-Motion: Decoupling Structure and Details via Discrete-Continuous Tokens for Human Motion Generation\n\nAuthors:Hequan Wang, Xuean Chen, Jiaxu Zhang, Zhengbo Zhang, Zhigang Tu\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled DC-Motion: Decoupling Structure and Details via Discrete-Continuous Tokens for Human Motion Generation, by Hequan Wang and 4 other authors\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:Text-to-motion generation requires modeling both global action structure and fine-grained motion dynamics from natural language. Existing approaches typically rely on either continuous diffusion models or vector-quantized discrete representations. Diffusion models generate smooth motions but lack explicit compositional structure for temporal planning, while discrete token-based methods improve controllability but compress motion into finite codebooks, losing fine-grained dynamics. We argue that this limitation stems from a representation mismatch: action semantics such as intent, phase transitions, and temporal layout are inherently discrete and compositional, whereas joint trajectories and motion dynamics are continuous and locally correlated. To address this, we propose DC-Motion, a discrete-continuous factorized framework for human motion generation. DC-Motion decomposes motion into discrete structural tokens capturing global action layout and continuous residual latents modeling fine-grained dynamics. A text-conditioned structure generator predicts discrete tokens via iterative masked modeling, and a diffusion-based residual generator produces continuous motion conditioned on the structure. Experiments on HumanML3D and KIT-ML demonstrate that DC-Motion achieves strong performance in both FID and R-Precision, outperforming representative diffusion-based and discrete-token baselines.\nCite as:  | arXiv:2606.14721 [cs.GR]\n   | (or arXiv:2606.14721v2 [cs.GR] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.14721 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Hequan Wang [view email]\n[v1] Thu, 28 May 2026 11:47:11 UTC (8,826 KB)\n[v2] Mon, 6 Jul 2026 14:01:50 UTC (1,198 KB)\n\nFull-text links:\n\n## Access Paper:\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled DC-Motion: Decoupling Structure and Details via Discrete-Continuous Tokens for Human Motion Generation, by Hequan Wang and 4 other authors\n\n  * View PDF\n  * HTML (experimental)\n  * TeX Source\n\nview license--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDC-Motion: Decoupling Structure and Details via Discrete-Continuous Tokens for Human Motion Generation — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/dc-motion-decoupling-structure-and-details-via-discrete)\nciteturn43742search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week;   * HumanML3D: DC-Motion achieved a Frechet Inception Distance (FID) of 0.041 (improving upon MoMask's 0.045) and a Top-1 R-Precision of 0.528, demonstrating superior motion quality and text-motion alignment. ...       eprint = {2606.14721},\n  * Quantization as a Decomposition Operator: Instead of viewing vector quantization purely as a lossy compression step, DC-Motion treats it as a structural filter. The codebook captures the \"macro\" layout of the motion, leaving the \"micro\" details to be cleanly handled by a continuous residual channel.\n  * Eased Diffusion Task: By conditioning the residual diffusion model on a pre-defined discrete structure, the diffusion model doesn't have to learn the entire motion distribution from scratch. It only needs to fill in the fine-grained details, making the generation task significantly more stable and efficient.\n\n## Key Results\n\nDC-Motion achieves state-of-the-art performance across both benchmarks, outperforming strong diffusion-based and discrete-token baselines:\n\n  * HumanML3D: DC-Motion achieved a Frechet Inception Distance (FID) of 0.041 (improving upon MoMask's 0.045) and a Top-1 R-Precision of 0.528, demonstrating superior motion quality and text-motion alignment.\n  * KIT-ML: The model achieved the lowest FID (0.148) and the best Top-1 R-Precision (0.442) among all compared methods.\n  * Qualitative Improvements: Visual results show that DC-Motion successfully generates smooth, realistic transitions for complex multi-stage actions (e.g., \"A person walks forward, then sits down\") without the boundary artifacts seen in discrete models or the structural failures seen in continuous diffusion models.\n\n## Limitations and Future Work\n\nNot discussed in the paper.\n\nBibTeX\n    \n    @misc{wang2026dc,\n      author = {Hequan Wang and Xuean Chen and Jiaxu Zhang and Zhengbo Zhang and Zhigang Tu},\n      title = {{DC-Motion: Decoupling Structure and Details via Discrete-Continuous Tokens for Human Motion Generation}},\n      year = {2026},\n      eprint = {2606.14721},\n      archivePrefix = {arXiv},\n      url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14721},\n    }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDC-Motion: Decoupling Structure and Details via Discrete-Continuous Tokens for Human Motion Generation | The Latest in AI (https://thelatestinai.com/papers/2606.14721)\nciteturn43742search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: last week; cs.GR·2606.14721 Copy arXiv ID·May 28, 2026 ... # DC-Motion: Decoupling Structure and Details via Discrete-Continuous Tokens for Human Motion Generation\n\ncs.GR·2606.14721 Copy arXiv ID·May 28, 2026\n\nSave\n\n# DC-Motion: Decoupling Structure and Details via Discrete-Continuous Tokens for Human Motion Generation\n\nAuthors: Hequan Wang, Xuean Chen, Jiaxu Zhang, Zhengbo Zhang, Zhigang Tu\n\nOrganizations: State Key Laboratory of Information Engineering in Surveying, Mapping and Remote Sensing, Wuhan University, Wuhan 430072, China · Geophysical Exploration Brigade of Hubei Provincial Geological Bureau, Wuhan 430058, China · Information Systems Technology and Design Pillar, Singapore University of Technology and Design, 487372, Singapore\n\nRead PDF ↗\n\n## Abstract\n\nText-to-motion generation requires modeling both global action structure and fine-grained motion dynamics from natural language. Existing approaches typically rely on either continuous diffusion models or vector-quantized discrete representations. Diffusion models generate smooth motions but lack explicit compositional structure for temporal planning, while discrete token-based methods improve controllability but compress motion into finite codebooks, losing fine-grained dynamics. We argue that this limitation stems from a representation mismatch: action semantics such as intent, phase transitions, and temporal layout are inherently discrete and compositional, whereas joint trajectories and motion dynamics are continuous and locally correlated. To address this, we propose DC-Motion, a discrete-continuous factorized framework for human motion generation. DC-Motion decomposes motion into discrete structural tokens capturing global action layout and continuous residual latents modeling fine-grained dynamics. A text-conditioned structure generator predicts discrete tokens via iterative masked modeling, and a diffusion-based residual generator produces continuous motion conditioned on the structure. Experiments on HumanML3D and KIT-ML demonstrate that DC-Motion achieves strong performance in both FID and R-Precision, outperforming representative diffusion-based and discrete-token baselines.\n\n## Paper details\n\nRead PDF ↗\n\nAsk AICite\n\nCopy citation\n\nBibTeXFor reference managers\n\nDOINot available for this paper\n\n### Resources\n\narXiv record\n\nTopics\n    \n\nText-to-Motion GenerationHuman Motion GenerationGenerative ModelingDiffusion Models for Motion